    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> a dictator was a lawful magistrate given supreme power for a single emergency and expected to lay it down as soon as the task was done.</li><li>Modern dictatorship, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> and the caudillos to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a>, Hitler and Stalin, means power held without legal limits or free elections and kept by repression and propaganda.</li><li>Political scientists divide dictatorships into personalist, military and one-party regimes, which differ in how they rule, how long they last and how they end.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The title once carried honour: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi">Giuseppe Garibaldi</a> officially styled himself “Dictator” of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/expedition-of-the-thousand">Expedition of the Thousand</a> in 1860, and leaders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/november-uprising">November Uprising</a> of 1830 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/january-uprising">January Uprising</a> of 1863 held the same title.</p></aside>
    </section>

