    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 501 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">By Roman tradition Titus Larcius is named the first dictator of the Republic</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">44 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> is made dictator for life and assassinated; the office is then abolished</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1799</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon Bonaparte</a> seizes power in the coup of 18 Brumaire, the model of Bonapartism</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1917</span><span class="rf-text">The Bolsheviks take power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, proclaiming a dictatorship of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/proletariat">proletariat</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1922</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Benito Mussolini</a> comes to power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and from 1925 builds the first fascist dictatorship</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1933</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a> becomes chancellor; the Enabling Act lets his cabinet make laws without the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1973</span><span class="rf-text">General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augusto-pinochet">Augusto Pinochet</a>’s coup overthrows Chile’s elected government</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1989</span><span class="rf-text">Revolutions end the communist dictatorships of Central and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="works">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Works</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/fascist-italy"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fascist-italy.jpg" alt="Fascist Italy" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/fascist-italy">Fascist Italy</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Benito_Mussolini_1930.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Keystone-France/Gamma-Keystone, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>A dictatorship is a government in which one leader, or a small group, holds absolute or near-absolute power. The political scientist Juan José Linz, defining authoritarian rule, named its features as power held by a single leader or a small group and exercised within ill-defined limits, limited political pluralism and limited mass mobilisation. Dictators rarely govern alone. They depend on an inner circle of military officers, party officials, security chiefs, friends and relatives, who carry out their orders and share in the rewards, and who are also the people best placed to overthrow them; a dictator must either satisfy this circle or replace its members. Opposition outside it is met with rewards, legal restrictions and repression, carried out by armies, police, paramilitary forces and secret police.</p><p>Most dictatorships keep the outward forms of constitutional government. They hold elections in which the opposition is not allowed to win, or not allowed to stand; legislatures meet but approve what they are given; courts sit but cannot overrule the ruler. Propaganda, censorship and control of information take the place of open debate. Since 1999, when the political scientist Barbara Geddes proposed the classification now widely used, scholars have distinguished dictatorships by where power lies. In military dictatorships officers decide who governs and shape policy; in one-party dictatorships the leadership of a ruling party does, and the party reaches into every level of society; in personalist dictatorships power rests with one individual, who fills offices with relatives and loyalists and values loyalty above competence. Many regimes mix these types. Totalitarian regimes, a term first used of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fascist-italy">Fascist Italy</a> in the 1920s, are dictatorships that use a single party, an official ideology, terror and control of the economy and mass media to try to remake society itself, not merely to rule it.</p><p>Dictatorship differs from the Roman office that gave it its name, a lawful magistracy granted for a single task and a limited time. It differs from absolute monarchy, in which power is also unlimited by law but rests on hereditary succession and long-established institutions, and it overlaps with tyranny, the classical term for one-man rule seized without right, which some scholars, such as the sociologist Daniel Chirot, still apply to modern dictators.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/julius-caesar.jpg" alt="Julius Caesar" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Retrato_de_Julio_C%C3%A9sar_(26724093101)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ángel M. Felicísimo from Mérida, España, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The word comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a>. After the expulsion of the kings, which tradition dates to about 509 BCE, the Romans divided royal power between two consuls elected each year, and for emergencies they created the dictator, originally called magister populi, “master of the infantry”, who held supreme command alone. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> names Titus Larcius, in 501 BCE, as the first dictator, though he also names Manius Valerius, and modern scholars put little faith in either account. Historians also debate whether the office was Roman or borrowed from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> cities, which had dictators of their own.</p><p>The dictator was the only important official of the Republic who was appointed rather than elected. One of the consuls named him, usually on the advice of the Senate and in a ritual held at night, for a specific purpose: most often to command in war, but also to hold elections, put down sedition, perform religious rites such as driving a nail into the wall of the Temple of Jupiter against pestilence, and once, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cannae">Battle of Cannae</a> in 216 BCE, to fill the depleted Senate. He chose a deputy, the master of the horse, and was attended by twenty-four lictors, whose bundles of rods kept their axes even inside the city, a sign of power over life and death. The other magistrates stayed in office under his authority, the Senate kept control of the treasury and the tribunes of the plebs kept at least some of their rights. Custom required him to resign as soon as his task was done: according to Livy, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cincinnatus">Lucius Quinctius Cincinnatus</a>, called from his plough in 458 BCE to rescue a Roman army, defeated the Aequi and laid down his office after sixteen days.</p><p>At least eighty-five dictators were appointed before the office lapsed after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-punic-war">Second Punic War</a>, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-fabius-maximus">Quintus Fabius Maximus</a>, whose delaying tactics against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> in 217 BCE earned him the name Cunctator, “the Delayer”. After 120 years of disuse it was revived in a new form by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Lucius Cornelius Sulla</a>, who in 82 BCE had himself appointed dictator to write laws and reorganise the state, with immunity for all his acts, and later resigned, and by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a>, who held the dictatorship repeatedly from 49 BCE and early in 44 BCE became dictator perpetuo, dictator for life. After Caesar’s murder the consul <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a> carried a law abolishing the office. Writers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niccolo-machiavelli">Niccolò Machiavelli</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-rousseau">Jean-Jacques Rousseau</a> later studied the Roman dictatorship as the model of emergency government.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="history">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Through History</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/napoleon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/napoleon.jpg" alt="Napoleon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jacques-Louis_David_-_The_Emperor_Napoleon_in_His_Study_at_the_Tuileries_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jacques-Louis David, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Some early modern regimes have been called dictatorships. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oliver-cromwell">Oliver Cromwell</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">Protectorate</a> of 1653–1658 was described as a military dictatorship by his enemies and by some modern scholars, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximilien-robespierre">Maximilien Robespierre</a> was called a dictator while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/committee-of-public-safety">Committee of Public Safety</a> directed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reign-of-terror">Reign of Terror</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1793–1794. Dictatorship became a major form of government in the 19th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon Bonaparte</a>’s coup of 18 Brumaire in 1799 and his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a>’s coup of 1851 gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> the models of Bonapartism and Caesarism: rule by a strong man who claimed to embody the nation and confirmed his power by plebiscite. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a> the wars of independence were followed by the rule of caudillos, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-gaspar-rodriguez-de-francia">José Gaspar Rodríguez de Francia</a>, perpetual dictator of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguay">Paraguay</a> from 1816, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porfirio-diaz">Porfirio Díaz</a>, who governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> for most of the years from 1876 to 1911.</p><p>The 20th century was the age of dictatorship. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> destroyed old monarchies and left new democracies unstable. The Bolsheviks seized power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1917 and created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a> had established personal and totalitarian rule by 1929; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Benito Mussolini</a> came to power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 1922 and from 1925 built the first fascist dictatorship; and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a>, appointed chancellor in January 1933, used emergency decrees and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enabling-act-of-1933">Enabling Act of 1933</a> to destroy German democracy within months. Dictators also ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jozef-pilsudski">Józef Piłsudski</a>’s coup of 1926, Portugal under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-de-oliveira-salazar">António de Oliveira Salazar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-franco">Francisco Franco</a> from 1939, much of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> and the Baltic states, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/getulio-vargas">Getúlio Vargas</a> from 1937. After Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>’s defeat in 1945 the Soviet Union imposed communist dictatorships across Central and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a>, and in 1949 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a> proclaimed the People’s Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a> dictatorship spread across the newly independent and developing world. Officers seized power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 1952, Brazil in 1964, Chile in 1973, when General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augusto-pinochet">Augusto Pinochet</a> overthrew the elected government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salvador-allende">Salvador Allende</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> in 1976, and repeatedly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, Ghana and many other African states; independence leaders banned rival parties; and personal rulers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mobutu-sese-seko">Mobutu Sese Seko</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaire">Zaire</a> (1965–1997) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-marcos">Ferdinand Marcos</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> (1965–1986) held power for decades. From the 1970s a wave of democratisation brought down the dictatorships of southern Europe, then many of those in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> America and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>; in 1989 the communist regimes of Central and Eastern Europe fell, and the Soviet Union itself dissolved in 1991.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="strengths">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Strengths</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/niccolo-machiavelli"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/niccolo-machiavelli.jpg" alt="Niccolò Machiavelli" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/niccolo-machiavelli">Niccolò Machiavelli</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Niccol%C3%B2_Machiavelli_by_Santi_di_Tito.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Santi di Tito, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The case for dictatorship began as a case for emergency government. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niccolo-machiavelli">Niccolò Machiavelli</a> argued in his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/discourses-on-livy">Discourses on Livy</a> that the Roman dictatorship had done the Republic good and not harm, because it was conferred by public authority, for a limited time and a limited purpose, and that republics without such a remedy for sudden dangers would be ruined. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-rousseau">Jean-Jacques Rousseau</a>, in The Social Contract (1762), accepted that in the gravest danger the laws might be suspended and a supreme ruler named, provided his term was short and could not be extended. For some three centuries the Roman office justified this trust: dictators were appointed, did their work and resigned, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cincinnatus">Cincinnatus</a> returning to his plough became a lasting model of civic virtue. In the 20th century the political scientist Clinton Rossiter argued in Constitutional Dictatorship (1948) that democracies facing war or economic collapse had been right to concentrate power in their executives, so long as the powers were temporary and the constitution survived them.</p><p>Modern dictators have claimed to bring order after chaos, unity after civil war and rapid development after stagnation, and their defenders can point to some evidence. Several <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> American caudillos are honoured as national heroes, and some dictatorships presided over rapid growth: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>’s “economic miracle” of 1959–1974 under Franco’s technocrats, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> under military and one-party rule in the 1960s and 1970s. Studies of economic growth find that dictatorships on average grow as fast as democracies, though with larger swings, and that rulers who feel secure in power are more likely to invest for the long term.</p><p>Political scientists have also found that some kinds of dictatorship are far more stable than others. One-party regimes, the most common kind of dictatorship since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, are less likely than other dictatorships to suffer civil war, insurgency or terrorism and tend to grow faster, because the party binds the elite together, carries information from the localities to the centre and provides for an orderly succession. Steven Levitsky and Lucan Way have shown that regimes born of social revolution, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and communist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, have proved exceptionally durable, with cohesive elites and loyal armed forces. Marxists from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Marx</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-lenin">Vladimir Lenin</a> defended the dictatorship of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/proletariat">proletariat</a> as a temporary stage needed to defend a revolution against its enemies.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="weaknesses">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Weaknesses</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/holocaust"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/holocaust.jpg" alt="Holocaust" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/holocaust">Holocaust</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Selection_on_the_ramp_at_Auschwitz-Birkenau,_1944_(Auschwitz_Album)_1b.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bernhard Walter, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The case against dictatorship rests above all on its record of violence. The communist and fascist dictatorships of the 20th century used cults of personality, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/concentration-camp">concentration camps</a>, forced labour, mass murder and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a>. Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> murdered some six million <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holocaust">Holocaust</a>, as well as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romani-people">Roma</a>, disabled people, Soviet prisoners of war and others; in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> some 680,000 people were shot in the Great Terror of 1937–1938, and millions passed through the camps of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulag">Gulag</a>; the famine caused by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-leap-forward">Great Leap Forward</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> killed tens of millions between 1959 and 1961; and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-rouge">Khmer Rouge</a> regime in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> caused the deaths of between 1.5 and 2 million people in less than four years. The military regimes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> and Chile made thousands of their opponents “disappear”.</p><p>Critics also point to failures built into the form. Because a dictator’s power rests on repression, subjects hide their true opinions and the ruler cannot know what they think, a problem political scientists call the dictator’s dilemma; without a free press or an opposition, bad news does not reach the top and mistakes go uncorrected. The economist Amartya Sen observed that no substantial famine has ever occurred in a democracy with a relatively free press. Studies find personalist dictatorships the most corrupt and repressive of the three types, since no institution can check the ruler, and Mobutu and Marcos became bywords for the plunder of their states. Research by Jessica Weeks and others finds personalist and military dictatorships more likely than democracies to start wars, since no voters or civilian elite can call their rulers to account, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saddam-hussein">Saddam Hussein</a>’s invasions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> in 1980 and Kuwait in 1990 and the Argentine junta’s invasion of the Falkland Islands in 1982 illustrate.</p><p>Succession is a further weakness. With no lawful way to replace the ruler, personalist dictatorships often collapse on the death of the dictator, and they are more likely than others to end in violence and less likely to give way to democracy; military regimes have usually been the shortest-lived, often ended by other officers. Liberal thinkers drew lessons from these regimes: Karl Popper’s The Open Society and Its Enemies (1945) defended democracy as the system that lets citizens remove their rulers without bloodshed, and Hannah Arendt’s The Origins of Totalitarianism (1951) described how ideology and terror destroyed every independent space in society. The Roman experience pointed the same way: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a>’s proscriptions and Caesar’s dictatorship for life showed how an office created to save the Republic could become an instrument for destroying it.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Impact &amp; Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi.jpg" alt="Giuseppe Garibaldi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi">Giuseppe Garibaldi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Giuseppe_Garibaldi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Salvatore Lo Forte, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Dictatorship gave modern politics a word whose meaning changed completely. The Roman dictator had been a lawful and honoured magistrate, and in the 19th century the title could still be borne with pride, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi">Giuseppe Garibaldi</a> bore it in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> in 1860; after the regimes of Hitler, Stalin and their imitators it became a term of condemnation, and few rulers have since called themselves dictators. The Roman idea of lawful emergency power survives in the emergency clauses of modern constitutions, such as Article 48 of the Weimar Constitution, which allowed the German president to govern by decree, and Article 16 of the French constitution of 1958. Government by presidential decree in the last years of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar-republic">Weimar Republic</a>, which smoothed Hitler’s path to power, is one reason why later constitutions surround such powers with safeguards. The German jurist Carl Schmitt distinguished in Die Diktatur (1921) between a “commissarial” dictatorship that defends an existing constitution and a “sovereign” dictatorship that creates a new one; his later service to the Nazi regime makes his work controversial.</p><p>The study of dictatorship became a field of its own. Hannah Arendt, Carl Friedrich and Zbigniew Brzezinski defined totalitarianism in the 1950s, and Juan Linz distinguished it from authoritarian regimes such as Franco’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, in which some pluralism survived. Historians have tested these models: Hans Mommsen and Ian Kershaw for Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and Sheila Fitzpatrick for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> found regimes far less monolithic than the theory implied, while others defend totalitarianism as a fair description of the regimes of Hitler, Stalin and Mao. Since the 1990s political scientists have studied how dictatorships survive and how they end, and Sergei Guriev and Daniel Treisman have described a newer type of “spin dictator”, who rules more by manipulating information than by terror.</p><p>Authoritarian government remains widespread, and by one count authoritarian regimes again outnumbered democracies by 2019. Communist parties govern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> as one-party states, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-korea">North Korea</a> has been led by three generations of the Kim family since 1948; Myanmar’s armed forces seized power in a coup in February 2021, and soldiers have taken power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burkina-faso">Burkina Faso</a> and Niger since 2020. Where dictatorships have ended, trials, truth commissions and memorials have recorded their crimes, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> trials of 1945–1946 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a>’s Trial of the Juntas in 1985.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Dictatorship began as a lawful Roman office, supreme power granted for a single emergency and surrendered when it passed. In the modern world the word came to mean rule without legal limits or free elections, by soldiers, single parties or one man; it was the form under which many of the 20th century’s mass killings took place, and it remains common today.</em></p>

